kubeos-kernel (6.12.117-1kubeos2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The build no longer depends on dpkg configuring initramfs-tools before the kernel. It
    declares Depends: linux-base, kmod, initramfs-tools | linux-initramfs-tool and its
    postinst runs update-initramfs -u -k all, so an initramfs for the KubeOS kernel is a
    guarantee rather than a race.
    Why it was needed, measured on the 2026-11-22 ISO build: the KubeOS kernel deb is built
    with make deb-pkg and declares NO Depends, so dpkg configured
    linux-image-6.12.117-kubeos 116 log lines BEFORE initramfs-tools; the kernel postinst
    then found no working initramfs-tools hook and the initramfs-tools trigger generated
    nothing (zero "update-initramfs: Generating" lines in build.log), so
    chroot/boot had no initrd.img-* and live-build's binary_syslinux failed the whole build
    with "ls: cannot access 'binary/live/initrd.img-*': No such file or directory".
    Debian's own kernel packages avoid this with the same Depends; since this package is what
    a KubeOS node and the ISO actually install, the guarantee lives here instead of requiring
    the kernel deb to be rebuilt.
  * Not the cause, for the record: the build log also downloads Debian's
    linux-image-6.12.117+deb13-amd64. That is the debian-installer pool build
    (installer_debian-installer, "apt-get ... --download-only"): the d-i environment needs a
    Debian kernel and downloads it into binary/pool. It is never installed - the live
    manifest carries only linux-image-6.12.117-kubeos - so no second kernel reached the ISO.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 01:20:00 +0530

kubeos-kernel (6.12.117-1kubeos1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * First release. KubeOS ships its own kernel (user decision 2026-11-22): the ISO installs
    this flavour meta and gets linux-image-6.12.117-kubeos from repo.kubeos.in instead of
    Debian's linux-image-amd64 from the trixie archive. The kernel version here always equals
    the kernel it depends on, so one number answers "which kernel does this node run".
  * Why the package is not called kubeos-kernel: live-build installs
    "${--linux-packages}-${--linux-flavours}" and later stages find the kernel by globbing
    vmlinuz-*<flavour>, so the flavour must be "kubeos" and --linux-packages "linux-image"
    yields this name - the same convention as linux-image-amd64.
  * Conflicts: linux-image-amd64, so a node cannot end up with a second, Debian kernel that
    wins the GRUB default at the next upgrade while the ISO's cmdline and initramfs were
    built for this one.
  * KNOWN LIMIT: the kernel is unsigned, so Secure Boot must be off on a KubeOS node. That is
    accepted for now (user decision 2026-11-22); signing it with a KubeOS key and MOK
    enrolment is a separate piece of work.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:30:00 +0530
